-- title:
--   Proof of Theorem 1, pp.28–29 — richness of consistent orderings
-- statement:
--   Fix a type $\theta_i$ of negotiator $i$ and write $\succeq$ for the reflexive-transitive closure of the relation generated by the instances of Definition 1.
--
--   1. For all bundles $a,c$: $a\,R\,c$ for every $R\in\Lambda(\theta_i)$ if and only if $a\succeq c$.
--   2. If $a\not\succeq c$, then some $R\in\Lambda(\theta_i)$ has $c\,P\,a$.
--   3. At the profile where both negotiators deem all alternatives acceptable, for every $b\in\mathbf B$ and every bundle $c\notin\mathbf B$ whose alternative in issue $X$ is not $o_X$, there are a negotiator $i$ and a consistent ordering $R_i$ with $b\,P_i\,c$.
--
--   This is what the proof means by "not unambiguously ranked", and it is the only way to produce an ordering in $\Lambda(\theta_i)$.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - generalization: one comparison, b against c, at the profile where all alternatives are acceptable → clause 3 is that comparison, for every b ∈ B rather than the rule's bundle at that profile; clauses 1 and 2 add the equivalence for every type and every pair of bundles; reason: the proof uses this general form wherever it says "unambiguously"; clause 3 is the page's sentence
--   - reading: a consistent extension map Λ; Definition 1 constrains it from one side and Theorem 1 does not name it → Λ(θ_i) is the set of all consistent orderings (admissibleOrderings); reason: it is the only reading under which Theorem 1 is true (singleton maps give a counterexample at m = 3) and the one the proof uses, pp.28–29
--   - reading: there exists a one-to-one mapping t_i : X → Y → the fixed map t(x_k) = y_{m+1−k}, t(o_X) = o_Y, for both negotiators; reason: Example 1 (pp.13–14) and the definition of B (p.14) fix it; t(o_X) is never used
--   - encoding: types in Θ_i, m+1 positions of o_X → the m rows and m columns of the paper's matrix, indexed by Fin m; reason: the paper's own domain M × M (p.15), which leaves out the type that accepts nothing
--   - encoding: indices 1, …, m, outside options o_X, o_Y, cells f_{ℓ−1,j}, f_{ℓ,j+1} → Fin m with value = index − 1, Option (Fin m) with none the outside option, Fin.rev for m+1−k, indices p, q with p + 1 = ℓ, q = j + 1; reason: Lean conventions; the instances are the same
-- source:
--   Kesten, Özyurt, Efficient and Incentive Compatible Mediation: An Ordinal Market Design Approach, working paper, 4 October 2018, proof of Theorem 1, 'if' part, pp.28–29

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_MultiIssueMediation_MediationRule
import Definitions.Def_MultiIssueMediation_UnanimousStep

namespace MultiIssueMediation

/-- **Richness of consistent orderings** (Kesten, Özyurt, *Efficient and Incentive Compatible
Mediation: An Ordinal Market Design Approach*, working paper, 4 October 2018, proof of Theorem 1,
'if' part, pp.28–29).

The first two clauses are the general form used throughout the proof: every ordering in `Λ(θ_i)`
ranks `a` weakly above `c` iff `a` reaches `c` along the instances of Definition 1, and otherwise
some ordering in `Λ(θ_i)` ranks `c` strictly above `a`.

The third clause is the page's sentence: at the profile where both negotiators deem all alternatives
acceptable (`t₁` is row `m`, `t₂` is column `1`), for `b ∈ B` and a bundle `c` that is neither a
logrolling bundle nor a bundle with the outside option in issue `X`, "there exists at least one
negotiator, `i`, and a consistent preference ordering, `R_i`, such that `b P_i c`". -/
theorem consistent_orderings_richness {m : ℕ} (hm : 2 ≤ m) :
    (∀ (i : Negotiator) (t : Fin m) (a c : Bundle m),
      (∀ R ∈ admissibleOrderings i t, R a c) ↔
        Relation.ReflTransGen (UnanimousStep i t) a c) ∧
      (∀ (i : Negotiator) (t : Fin m) (a c : Bundle m),
        ¬ Relation.ReflTransGen (UnanimousStep i t) a c →
          ∃ R ∈ admissibleOrderings i t, StrictlyPrefers R c a) ∧
      (∀ (t₁ t₂ : Fin m), t₁.val + 1 = m → t₂.val = 0 →
        ∀ b ∈ logrollBundles m, ∀ c : Bundle m, c ∉ logrollBundles m → c.1 ≠ none →
          ∃ i : Negotiator, ∃ R ∈ admissibleOrderings i (typeAt i t₁ t₂),
            StrictlyPrefers R b c) := by sorry

end MultiIssueMediation
